Create Group 02 — Form Controls for the DevKit.

This group belongs to:

Layer 01 — Foundations & Interface Primitives

Group 01 — Interaction Controls already exists.

Use the DevKit Core Design Constitution as the immutable global foundation.

Do not redefine the global design system.

Inherit all:
- colors
- semantic colors
- typography
- spacing
- control heights
- radius
- borders
- elevation
- motion
- responsive breakpoints
- accessibility rules
- focus rules
- touch-target rules
- density rules
- themes
- RTL rules
- localization rules

The purpose of this group is to provide the fundamental reusable controls through which users enter, edit, search, verify, select, and manipulate textual, numeric, temporal, and sensitive information.

Create exactly these 10 components:

011 Text Input
012 Textarea
013 Search Field
014 Number Input
015 Password Input
016 OTP / Verification Input
017 Date Input
018 Time Input
019 Date & Time Input
020 Input Group

These components must be production-grade primitives.

Do not create:
- form pages
- registration pages
- checkout forms
- profile pages
- settings pages
- application-specific forms
- multi-step forms

Those are higher-level patterns and must be composed from these primitives.

Every component must support the DevKit's:
- Light theme
- Dark theme
- High Contrast compatibility
- Compact density
- Comfortable density
- Spacious density
- LTR
- RTL
- localization
- dynamic text scaling
- reduced motion
- reduced transparency
- keyboard
- mouse
- touch
- stylus
- screen readers

Every component must have appropriate:
- default state
- hover state where applicable
- focus state
- filled state
- disabled state
- read-only state
- error state
- warning state where applicable
- success state where applicable
- loading state where applicable

Every component must correctly handle:
- empty value
- short content
- long content
- localized content
- invalid content
- pasted content
- autofill
- browser/platform input behavior
- keyboard behavior
- accessibility labels
- help text
- validation messages
- required fields
- optional fields

Do not communicate validation state through color alone.

Use semantic:
- labels
- icons
- messages
- states
- accessible descriptions

Every form control must preserve its dimensions when validation or loading content appears where possible.

Prevent layout jumping.

Use the global control height tokens:

XS = 28px
SM = 32px
MD = 40px
LG = 48px
XL = 56px

Default form control:
MD = 40px.

Touch interaction must provide a minimum effective target of 44×44px.

Use the global 4px spatial foundation and approved spacing tokens.

Default relationship:

Label → control = 6px

Control → help/error message = 6px

Field → field = 16px

Related field group = 24px

Form section = 32px

Use the global typography hierarchy.

Default field text:
16px / 24px.

Do not use arbitrary font sizes.

Use semantic border and focus tokens.

Default border:
1px.

Focus:
2px accessible focus treatment.

Do not use heavy borders.

Do not use excessive shadows.

Do not use unnecessary gradients.

Do not use decorative glass effects inside ordinary form controls.

The interface should feel:
precise,
calm,
premium,
clear,
responsive,
tactile,
and effortless.

The user should immediately understand:
- what the field is
- what they should enter
- whether the field is required
- whether the current value is valid
- what went wrong
- what to do next

Do not rely on placeholder text as the permanent field label.

Placeholder text must be supplementary.

Every input must support an accessible programmatic label.

On mobile:
- use appropriate native input behavior
- trigger the correct virtual keyboard
- maintain 44px minimum touch interaction
- avoid unnecessary horizontal compression
- preserve readable text
- prevent controls from being hidden behind the keyboard
- transform compound controls when necessary

On desktop:
- support mouse
- keyboard navigation
- autofill
- copy/paste
- shortcuts where appropriate
- hover feedback where meaningful

The component architecture must separate:
label
control
leading affordance
input content
trailing affordance
help text
validation message
character count
status

where appropriate.

Do not force every component to display every element.

Only display elements that provide meaningful information.

All validation must support:
client-side feedback
server-side feedback
asynchronous validation where appropriate

Do not validate aggressively while the user is still typing unless the validation is clearly useful.

Support appropriate validation timing:
- on blur
- on submit
- on change for specific immediate constraints
- asynchronous validation for server-dependent conditions

Avoid unnecessary error flashing.

All components must support controlled and uncontrolled usage where the underlying framework allows it.

All components must expose predictable values and events.

All components must be composable with:
Label
Help Text
Error Message
Description
Button
Icon Button
Form Group
Field Group
and future DevKit form components.

